WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.760
A biconditional establishes both directions between two conditions.

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.175
Each is sufficient for the other and necessary for the other.

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.760
A route is listed if and only if it has been surveyed.

4
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.599
If the route is listed, it has been surveyed.

5
00:00:14.899 --> 00:00:17.075
If it has been surveyed, it is listed.

6
00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:19.209
Those are two different guarantees.

7
00:00:19.509 --> 00:00:26.549
They also settle the absent cases: an unlisted route is unsurveyed, and an unsurveyed route is unlisted.

8
00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:30.775
The rule alone does not establish that any route has either status.

9
00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:36.429
When a sentence really establishes both directions, reversing the relationship is allowed.

10
00:00:36.729 --> 00:00:41.999
The task is to find the second guarantee in the wording rather than assume it from familiarity.

11
00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:47.355
Read the if contribution and the only-if contribution as two ordinary rules.

12
00:00:47.655 --> 00:00:52.092
Check that both appear in the sentence before replacing them with a double arrow.

13
00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:57.405
Before reversing a relationship, point to the wording that supplies the reverse guarantee.

14
00:00:57.705 --> 00:01:07.113
A biconditional permits both directions; it does not remove restrictions, prove existence or turn a compound side into separate sufficient routes.
